From 0061f0a96fc8c116c26c4a4da6cd7afd8339e490 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Dawid=20Wr=C3=B3bel?= <me@dawidwrobel.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 10:45:23 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 21/41] tee: qseecom: do not let a session outlive the
 application it names

Unloading on load-session close left every client session naming that
application holding a bare id, and TZ hands out application ids as small
integers and reuses them. After an unload and any later load, a stale session
addressed a different trusted application entirely -- an unprivileged client's
commands delivered to one it never opened. The comment shipped alongside said
those sessions were 'unaffected', which was the opposite of true.

Give each registry entry a generation that is never reused, record it in the
session that resolved it, and check it on invoke. Generation zero means the
session named an application the boot chain loaded, which this driver cannot
unload and which therefore cannot be replaced underneath it, so those keep
working.

Correct both comments that asserted the broken invariant.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
---
 drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c | 90 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 77 insertions(+), 13 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c b/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c
index f335e06b0..8ca1b6517 100644
--- a/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c
